#{[17592186338872 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338872 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338872 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338872 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+4t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=7-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cadba95b-e6bb-5c0e-9d1a-07af961b4052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338872 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4t \\\\ -3+5t \\\\ 7-6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(2,-3,7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,5,-6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{5}=\\frac{z-7}{-6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338876 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338876 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338876 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338876 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338876 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a15416a-0d95-55a8-b911-87d641acee2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338876 #edb/ref 1074 "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(10,0,-6)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨3,-7,-5⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y-0}{-7}=\\frac{z-(-6)}{-5}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z+6}{-5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338878 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338878 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338878 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(9,3,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨4,-5,3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338878 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "489a2b97-718c-5e30-886b-642db6f4e1ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338878 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338880 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338880 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338880 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338880 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d55a1be6-a075-557b-b7ae-a0512fb81218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338880 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6+10t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
